D={
"Rosa Parks":"Rosa Parks (1913–2005) was a seamstress and secretary of the Montgomery branch of the NAACP in Alabama, where segregation laws forced Black passengers to give up bus seats to white riders. On 1 December 1955 she refused, and was arrested. Her arrest sparked the Montgomery bus boycott, led by the young pastor Martin Luther King Jr., which lasted 381 days until the Supreme Court ruled bus segregation unconstitutional. She faced job loss and threats, moved to Detroit, and was later called 'the mother of the civil rights movement'.",
"Emmeline Pankhurst":"Emmeline Pankhurst (1858–1928) founded the Women's Social and Political Union in Manchester in 1903 with her daughters, adopting the motto 'Deeds, not words'. Suffragettes smashed windows, chained themselves to railings and committed arson; jailed, they went on hunger strike and were force-fed. She suspended the campaign during World War I to support the war effort. Women over 30 won the vote in 1918, and women gained equal voting rights in 1928, weeks after her death.",
"Maximilien Robespierre":"Maximilien Robespierre (1758–1794), a provincial lawyer inspired by Rousseau, became a leader of the radical Jacobins during the French Revolution, known as 'the Incorruptible'. He opposed the death penalty early on but argued for executing Louis XVI. As the leading member of the Committee of Public Safety, he oversaw the Reign of Terror (1793–94), in which about 17,000 were officially executed, claiming that 'terror is nothing but prompt, severe, inflexible justice'. Fearing they would be next, his colleagues arrested him on 9 Thermidor (27 July 1794), and he was guillotined the next day.",
"Giuseppe Garibaldi":"Giuseppe Garibaldi (1807–1882), a sailor from Nice, joined Mazzini's Young Italy movement, was sentenced to death and fled to South America, where he fought in wars in Brazil and Uruguay. Returning, he defended the Roman Republic in 1849. In 1860 he led about 1,000 volunteer 'Redshirts' to conquer Sicily and Naples, then handed them to King Victor Emmanuel II, completing much of Italian unification. Admired worldwide, he inspired Indian nationalists such as Lala Lajpat Rai, who wrote his biography.",
"Suleiman the Magnificent":"Suleiman I (1494–1566), the tenth Ottoman sultan, reigned for 46 years, the longest of any sultan. He captured Belgrade (1521), Rhodes (1522), defeated Hungary at Mohács (1526), besieged Vienna (1529), and took Baghdad and much of North Africa; his admiral Barbarossa dominated the Mediterranean. Known in Turkey as Kanuni ('the Lawgiver'), he codified laws on taxes and land. He married the former slave Hürrem Sultan (Roxelana), who became very powerful. His architect Mimar Sinan built the Süleymaniye Mosque. He died during a campaign in Hungary.",
"Saladin":"Salah ad-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub (1137–1193) was a Kurdish officer who became vizier of Fatimid Egypt in 1169, then abolished the Fatimid caliphate and founded the Ayyubid dynasty. He united Egypt, Syria and parts of Iraq, and on 4 July 1187 destroyed the crusader army at the Horns of Hattin, then took Jerusalem, allowing most Christians to ransom themselves. He fought Richard the Lionheart in the Third Crusade to a truce that kept Jerusalem in Muslim hands. Even European writers praised his chivalry and generosity; he died almost penniless.",
"Kublai Khan":"Kublai Khan (1215–1294), grandson of Genghis Khan, became Great Khan in 1260 after a civil war with his brother. He moved the capital to Khanbaliq (Dadu, today's Beijing), adopted a Chinese dynastic name, Yuan (1271), and completed the conquest of the Song in 1279, uniting China. He extended the Grand Canal, issued paper money, and welcomed foreigners such as Marco Polo. His invasions of Japan (1274, 1281), Vietnam and Java failed. He patronised Tibetan Buddhism through the lama Phagpa, who designed a script for Mongolian.",
"Timur":"Timur (1336–1405), known in Europe as Tamerlane from 'Timur the Lame' after injuries to his right leg and arm, was a Turco-Mongol from near Samarkand. He conquered Persia, Mesopotamia, the Caucasus and the Golden Horde, sacked Delhi in 1398, and defeated the Ottoman sultan Bayezid I at Ankara (1402). His campaigns, marked by massacres and pyramids of skulls in cities like Isfahan and Baghdad, killed an estimated 17 million people. He brought craftsmen from conquered lands to make Samarkand magnificent, building the Bibi-Khanym mosque. He died while marching to invade China. His descendant Babur founded the Mughal Empire.",
"Aurangzeb":"Muhiuddin Muhammad Aurangzeb (1618–1707) defeated and executed his brothers, including the scholarly Dara Shikoh, and imprisoned his father Shah Jahan, becoming emperor in 1658. He ruled the Mughal Empire at its largest, conquering Bijapur (1686) and Golconda (1687). A devout Sunni, he reimposed the jizya on non-Muslims (1679), banned music at court, destroyed some temples including at Mathura and Varanasi, and executed the Sikh Guru Tegh Bahadur (1675). He spent his last 26 years fighting the Marathas in the Deccan, and after his death in 1707 the empire fragmented. Historians debate how much his policies were driven by religion versus politics.",
"Humayun":"Nasir-ud-din Muhammad Humayun (1508–1556), Babur's son, became emperor in 1530 but faced rebellious brothers and the Afghan Sher Shah Suri, who defeated him at Chausa (1539) and Kannauj (1540). He fled through Sindh, where his son Akbar was born, and took refuge with the Safavid Shah Tahmasp in Persia. With Persian help he recaptured Kabul and then Delhi in 1555, but died in 1556 after falling down the stairs of his library. His tomb in Delhi, built by his widow, inspired the Taj Mahal.",
"Sher Shah Suri":"Sher Shah Suri (1486–1545), born Farid Khan, was an Afghan who earned the name 'Sher' by killing a tiger. Rising in Bihar, he defeated Humayun and ruled north India from 1540. In five years he reorganised administration, measured land for fair revenue, introduced the silver rupiya (the origin of the rupee), and rebuilt the road from Bengal to Kabul (later the Grand Trunk Road) with sarais and trees. He died in an explosion while besieging Kalinjar fort. His tomb stands in Sasaram.",
"Nur Jahan":"Mehr-un-Nissa (1577–1645), born to a Persian family in Kandahar, married Emperor Jahangir in 1611 and received the title Nur Jahan ('light of the world'). She effectively ruled alongside him, issuing farmans (orders), having coins struck in her name, and controlling trade. She hunted tigers, designed fabrics and gardens, and built her father's tomb, Itimad-ud-Daulah, in Agra. After Jahangir's death, her son-in-law lost the succession to Shah Jahan, and she retired to Lahore.",
"Prithviraj Chauhan":"Prithviraj III (about 1166–1192), the Chauhan king of Ajmer and Delhi, fought neighbours including the Chandelas and the Gahadavala king Jaichand of Kannauj; legend says he eloped with Jaichand's daughter Samyukta. In 1191 he defeated Muhammad of Ghor at the first Battle of Tarain, but in 1192 Ghor returned and defeated him; he was captured and killed. The later epic Prithviraj Raso by Chand Bardai romanticised him as the last Hindu emperor of Delhi.",
"Alauddin Khalji":"Alauddin Khalji (about 1266–1316) raided the rich Yadava capital of Devagiri in 1296, then murdered his uncle Jalaluddin and seized the throne. He repelled several large Mongol invasions, built the fort of Siri, and imposed price controls on grain and goods, with markets supervised by officials and a land tax of half the produce. He conquered Gujarat, Ranthambore and Chittor (1303) — the siege later romanticised in the legend of Rani Padmini — and sent Malik Kafur on raids deep into south India. After his death his empire quickly fell into chaos.",
"Muhammad bin Tughlaq":"Muhammad bin Tughlaq (about 1290–1351) was a learned, generous but erratic sultan of Delhi. He raised taxes in the Doab during a famine, moved the capital and much of Delhi's population to Daulatabad in the Deccan, then back again, and issued copper token coins that were easily forged, causing chaos. He planned conquests of Khurasan and Qarachil that failed. Ibn Battuta, who served him as a judge, described both his generosity and his cruelty. Revolts during his reign created the Bahmani Sultanate and Vijayanagara.",
"Pulakeshin II":"Pulakeshin II (reigned about 610–642) of the Chalukyas of Badami seized power from his uncle and expanded his kingdom across the Deccan. Around 618 he defeated Harsha on the Narmada, stopping Harsha's southward expansion. His court poet Ravikirti's Aihole inscription (634) records his victories. Xuanzang visited and praised his warriors. He fought the Pallavas, but in 642 Narasimhavarman I captured Badami and Pulakeshin was probably killed.",
"Chandragupta II":"Chandragupta II (reigned about 375–415), son of Samudragupta, is identified with the legendary King Vikramaditya. He married Kuberanaga and allied with the Vakatakas by marrying his daughter Prabhavatigupta to their king. He defeated the Western Kshatrapas (Shakas) of Gujarat and Malwa, gaining Arabian Sea ports and earning the title 'Shakari'. The iron pillar at Delhi, which has not rusted for 1,600 years, may commemorate him. The Chinese monk Faxian visited his peaceful empire, and Kalidasa may have been at his court.",
"Nebuchadnezzar II":"Nebuchadnezzar II (about 634–562 BCE), the greatest king of the Neo-Babylonian Empire, defeated the Egyptians at Carchemish (605 BCE). When Judah rebelled, he captured Jerusalem in 597 BCE and destroyed the city and Solomon's Temple in 587 BCE, deporting its people to Babylon. He rebuilt Babylon with massive walls, the blue-glazed Ishtar Gate (now in Berlin) and the Etemenanki ziggurat, possibly the Tower of Babel; legend credits him with the Hanging Gardens for his Median wife.",
"Darius I":"Darius I (about 550–486 BCE) seized the Persian throne in 522 BCE after killing a usurper, recording his victory in the trilingual Behistun inscription, key to deciphering cuneiform. He divided the empire into about 20 satrapies, standardised taxes and the gold daric coin, built the Royal Road and a canal from the Nile to the Red Sea, and began Persepolis. He conquered the Indus valley, which paid the largest tribute. He crushed the Ionian Revolt, but his army was defeated at Marathon (490 BCE).",
"Tutankhamun":"Tutankhamun (about 1341–1323 BCE) became pharaoh at about nine; he was probably the son of Akhenaten. Under his advisers Ay and Horemheb, he restored the worship of Amun and the old gods and moved the court back from Amarna. He died at about 19, possibly from an infection after a leg fracture and malaria. His small tomb in the Valley of the Kings, discovered almost intact by Howard Carter in 1922, contained over 5,000 objects, including the golden death mask, making him the most famous pharaoh today.",
"Constantine the Great":"Constantine I (272–337) was proclaimed emperor by troops at York in 306. Before the Battle of the Milvian Bridge (312) he reportedly saw a cross with the words 'In this sign conquer'; after victory he favoured Christianity, and with Licinius issued the Edict of Milan (313) granting religious freedom. He called the Council of Nicaea (325) to settle the Arian controversy, became sole emperor in 324, and founded Constantinople in 330 as the new capital. He introduced the gold solidus and was baptised on his deathbed.",
"Marcus Aurelius":"Marcus Aurelius (121–180), the last of Rome's 'Five Good Emperors', ruled from 161. His reign saw the Antonine Plague and long wars against the Marcomanni and other Germanic tribes on the Danube frontier. In his tent he wrote the Meditations, private notes in Greek on Stoic philosophy, urging self-discipline, duty and acceptance of fate. He made his son Commodus heir, ending the era of adoptive succession, and Commodus's misrule followed.",
"Catherine the Great":"Catherine II (1729–1796) was a German princess, Sophie of Anhalt-Zerbst, who married the future Peter III of Russia. In 1762 she overthrew her husband in a palace coup (he was killed soon after) and ruled for 34 years. She expanded Russia by annexing Crimea, defeating the Ottomans, and partitioning Poland. She corresponded with Voltaire and Diderot, founded the Hermitage collection and the Smolny Institute for girls, but strengthened serfdom and crushed Pugachev's rebellion (1773–75).",
}
